    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-17126",
      "knowledge_point_id": "87f80908-037b-56ae-a2b5-59bdc49f0acb",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given a Description of the Radius and Angle",
      "problem": "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $2,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$",
      "worked_solution": "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=2,\\,\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\ x & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\ y & =2(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2}).$",
      "answer_fields": [
