    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-276807",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ad049d53-af38-5917-b9d0-be965cadabcb",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given Some Information About Another Point",
      "problem": "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).$ Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(2\\sqrt{3},2).$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot 4,\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=(8,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-4\\sqrt{3},-4).$",
